      en: "Tel Tsaf, located in the Jordan Valley 5 km south of Beit She'an, is a major Late Chalcolithic site (c. 5000–4500 BCE) in the southern Levant. Excavations directed by Yosef Garfinkel revealed monumental mudbrick buildings, storage rooms, and a 3-meter-wide gate. Finds indicate long-distance trade, including copper objects and ivory from Egypt. Notable are sealed clay balls with impressions, interpreted as an early accounting system. The settlement was surrounded by a stone wall. Tel Tsaf provides crucial evidence for social complexity before the Bronze Age.",
